  Given the answers — client-only, tasks defined here, due-date reminders, layered in-page + OS delivery — one real fork
  remains: [1mhow a reminder is represented.[0m

  [1mA. Derived reminders (recommended).[0m No notification objects. A pure function [38;5;153mdueReminders(tasks, now, dismissed) → [39m
  [38;5;153mReminder[][39m recomputes from the task list on every tick. The only persisted notification state is a set of dismissed
  keys ([38;5;153mtaskId:threshold[39m). Edit a due date and reminders self-correct with no reconciliation code. Testable with a fake
  [38;5;153mnow[39m and no DOM. Cost: no history of past reminders, and dismissed keys need pruning when tasks are deleted.

  [1mB. Materialized notification records.[0m Crossing a threshold writes [38;5;153m{id, taskId, kind, firedAt, readAt}[39m to storage. Buys
  history and read/unread, and it's the shape a multi-user backend would want. Cost: records go stale the moment a
  task's due date or status changes, so you need reconciliation logic — and that's the bulk of the bugs in this kind of
  system. It's also most of the activity-feed machinery we just declined.

  [1mC. Per-task [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m timers.[0m Arm a timer per upcoming reminder instead of polling. Sounds precise, but timers die on
  reload, browsers throttle them hard in background tabs, and every task edit means re-arming. More moving parts, worse
  reliability.

  [1mRecommendation: A[0m, with a single ~30s tick plus a recompute on load and after any task mutation. A 30s tick is free at
  this scale and is the thing that survives laptop sleep, which timers don't. B is the one to revisit if a backend ever
  arrives — and going A→B later is a contained change, because the UI already consumes a [38;5;153mReminder[][39m.

  Does A sound right, or do you want history/read-unread from the start?

